  "eventMetadata": {
    "context_description": "Recent U.S. labor market data have tilted trader consensus toward no change at the September 16 FOMC meeting. July nonfarm payrolls contracted by 23,000, the unemployment rate eased to 4.1 percent, and subsequent inflation readings came in softer than expected, easing concerns about reacceleration tied to prior energy shocks. These prints followed the July FOMC decision to hold the federal funds rate at 3.50\u20133.75 percent despite three dissents favoring a hike, reducing immediate pressure for action. With the August employment report due September 4 and additional inflation data still ahead, markets price the status quo at 60.5 percent while assigning 39.5 percent odds to a 25-basis-point increase, reflecting a close call driven by mixed signals on growth and price pressures.",
    "context_requires_regen": true,
